  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$",
  "worked_solution": "Note that the function $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ is not defined at the points where $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.$ On the interval ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ the excluded values are $x={90}^{∘},\\,{270}^{∘}.$ \n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=0,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-1=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=0.$ Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ so it is never equal to zero. Therefore, this equation has no solutions.\n\n- Then, we solve $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-1=0,$ or equivalently, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ The solutions to this equation for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$  are $x={30}^{∘},\\,{150}^{∘}.$\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={30}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={150}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=4\\,500.\n$$",
